<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!-- The captured pieces and the balance of the material, in the left margin of the view (x 0 to 100; the board is 100 to 740). A canvas of the SceneView space with no safe area,
     like BoardCoordinates: it is at fixed scene units next to the board, and ChessMaterialComponent decides what a narrow window with insets leaves of it. Layer 5: over the pieces (0)
     and the result bar (1), under the HUD (10) and the panels (20); the component is listed before the canvas so that it gives the variables their words before the canvas draws in the
     same frame. Nothing here takes a click (no blocksInput, nothing interactive, not modal), so the board and the Reset button get every press. The right margin is left free.
     Two groups mirrored about the middle of the board: the lower one (MaterialBottom*, y 500) is what the color at the bottom of the board has taken, the upper one (MaterialTop*, y 84)
     what the other color has taken; they swap when the board turns. A group is a list of five rows in the order queen, rook, bishop, knight, pawn, each a chip with the picture of a piece
     (a UIImage whose sprite the component sets: the pieces of the other color than the group's) and the count "x3"; a row of a piece that has not been taken is hidden and takes no room, so the
     rows pack at the top of the list. A list is 64 x 216 at x 8 (5 x 40 + 4 x 4); the box of the two lists, (8, 84) to (72, 716), is the one of Game/MaterialLayout.h, which says that the rows are shown only while the
     box lies inside the safe area (in the narrowest case of the size matrix, a 480 px window with side insets, the safe area starts at about x = 75.6 and about 24 units of the margin are left). The balance, "+3" on the side that is ahead and nothing
     on the other, is a box of 23 x 28 units at the board's edge (x 77 to 100) whose AutoFit shrinks the text (to 12 units at the smallest, which is 7 px at the smallest fit scale, 0.571: the sizes are whole pixels and the smallest is rounded up) instead of cutting it, and it is never hidden. The names all start with
     Material because elements are found by name in every canvas of the scene. The chip is a cream a little darker than the light squares (so that a test can tell a chip from a square), the text
     a dark brown that is not one of the two colors of the board's labels. The look (the colors, the sizes of the font, the pictures at this size) is a first guess nobody has seen in a window.
     The numbers above are those of the default view. In the Portrait view (the variant for orientation:portrait; the board is at (40, 144) to (680, 784)) the two lists are horizontal: the upper group
     above the board at (40, 92), 336 x 40 (5 x 64 + 4 x 4; a horizontal list stretches the 40-unit height to its rows, which take a width of 64 here), the lower group under it at (40, 792); they
     mirror and swap with the turn of the board as before. Their box, (40, 92) to (376, 832), is the Portrait one of Game/MaterialLayout.h (the rows show while it lies inside the safe area).
     A balance is a 48 x 40 box at x 384, beside the end of its list, never hidden.
     The MaterialPanel* elements are a second set of the same two groups for the Wide view, drawn in the bands of the move panel (ChessMovePanel.xml, the box (760, 80) to (1080, 720)): the upper
     group at y 88 in MovePanelBandTopPlayer (80 to 136), the lower one at y 608 in MovePanelBandBottomPlayer (600 to 656), each a horizontal list of five chips of 52 x 40 from x 768 (the inner
     edge of the box) and its balance in a 32 x 40 box ending at 1072. They bind the same variables and their icons get the same sprites. ChessMaterialComponent shows exactly one set: this
     one while the move panel is shown (MovePanelFits, the rule of the panel itself, so the material never goes with a hidden panel), the margin set otherwise; the hidden set's lists and
     balances are hidden whole. The longest row (eight pawns, two knights, bishops and rooks, the queen, "+39") fits the 304 inner units at the smallest Wide scale. -->
